            text: 'The Hawaiian Kingdom illustrates a different path: a Native Hawaiian monarchy adopted written constitutional institutions while retaining royal authority and a distinct Indigenous political community. The 1840 constitutional design included a king, island governors, a House of Nobles, a House of Representatives and a supreme court. Newbury’s study describes continuing patronage and a gradual bureaucratic formation, while Osorio’s history emphasizes how Western law reshaped national identity and political power. A constitutional form therefore records institutional change without proving that authority had become equally distributed or free from colonial pressure.',
