            type: 'paragraph',
            text: 'Pakistan shows a Sunni constitutional arrangement in which Islamic supremacy and representative institutions are layered rather than fused into one clerical office. The consolidated Constitution provides for an Islamic Republic, an Islamic Council that advises the political branches, and a Federal Shariat Court that may examine legislation for repugnancy to Islamic injunctions. Lombardi’s comparison cautions that the design and independence of the reviewing institution shape what a Sharia guarantee does in practice; the Pakistani case therefore belongs to the category of constitutional theocracy or religious-constitutional hybrid only with its period, institutional limits, military interruptions and contested interpretation kept visible.',
