            text: 'The CDU’s first postwar documents sought a new political beginning after Nazism by gathering Catholic, Protestant, democratic, and social forces into a broad party. The 26 June Berlin appeal, the 1 July Kölner Leitsätze, the December 1945 Bad Godesberg meeting, and the later British-zone programmes show both convergence and unresolved regional and economic differences. The KAS chronology records the 1949 election and the federal party’s completion at Goslar in 1950. This sequence helps explain how Christian democracy moved from founding rhetoric and regional organization toward a national people’s party; it does not turn one zone’s programme into the settled view of every CDU/CSU member or subsequent government.',
