            text: 'The Carnation Revolution opened a transition from Estado Novo to competitive party politics. The CDS declaration of 19 July 1974 described a pluralist, law-bound and non-totalitarian democracy, and its July 1975 constitutional project presented personalist democracy, solidarity, pluralism and dignity alongside a specifically Portuguese socialist path. Portuguese scholarship treats Christian-democratic parties and Catholic hierarchy as distinct actors whose relationships with the new state and early elections had to be negotiated. The 1976 Constitution established a republican democratic framework with parliamentary oversight, freedom of conscience and religion, church–state separation, and non-confessional public education. Official revision history records the 1982 abolition of the Council of the Revolution and creation of a Constitutional Court, followed in 1989 by the end of the irreversibility of earlier nationalisations. The CDS’s official history records participation in the 1979–1980 Democratic Alliance governments, while Duarte describes later CDS–PP ideological plasticity. This sequence documents changing party projects and constitutional settlements, not uniform Catholic influence, a single party-authored constitution, or an uninterrupted present-day ideology.',
