      editorialNote: 'The entry treats Christian democracy as a plural and historically changing family. It distinguishes personalist and social-market arguments from clerical rule, theocracy, Christian nationalism, generic conservatism, social democracy, and religious socialism. The Chile addition is a bounded Latin American case: a protected 1965 party publication, an official Spanish-language library overview, and Spanish-, English-, and Portuguese-language scholarship are used to describe the 1964–1970 Revolución en Libertad programme and its agrarian, educational, participatory, and electoral tensions. The Italian addition is a bounded 1943–1948 transition: an Italian primary programme, the Senate’s official constitutional record, and Forlenza and Thomassen’s scholarly interpretation are kept distinct, and the 1948 settlement is not attributed to Christian Democracy alone. This pass adds a bounded Swiss case from the post-Sonderbund Catholic-conservative tradition through the CVP/PDC and its national 2020–2021 transition into The Centre, using German HLS scholarship, French parliamentary history, a dated federal party profile, and an open-access political-data report. This pass extends the bounded Portuguese 1974–1989 transition case with the CDS’s 1975 constitutional project, official parliamentary revision history, CDS coalition chronology, and Portuguese scholarship on later ideological plasticity and religious-populist discourse. This pass adds a bounded Brazilian 1945–1965 PDC case using the official Portuguese TSE registry and programme record, Coelho’s Portuguese-language programme-and-speech study, and Souza’s Portuguese study of lay Catholic actors and French-humanist/Catholic-social intellectual networks. This pass adds a bounded Austrian April 1945 ÖVP formation case using an official Austrian institutional history, the German 1945 party programme, and Corduwener’s scholarly account of postwar Christian-democratic people’s parties. This pass adds a bounded Venezuelan 1946–1998 COPEI case using official Spanish-language party self-description and English-language comparative scholarship on Latin American diffusion, Puntofijo-era party competition, social bases, democratic stability, and the 1998 collapse of established party brands. The MRP addition is a bounded French-language case using party-affiliated retrospective history, French scholarship on doctrine and organization, a regional press-network study, and an abstract-level English study of colonial policy; it distinguishes self-description, organization, parliamentary role, and colonial practice and does not establish a present-day France score. The Austrian sources distinguish party formation, party self-description, comparative interpretation, and implementation; they do not settle continuity with the interwar Christian Social or authoritarian past, membership, voter composition, or present-day country classification. The Brazilian sources distinguish legal and party self-description, programme change, electoral and legislative behavior, and scholarly interpretation; they do not establish uniform implementation, membership, Brazilian Catholic politics as a whole, or continuity with later parties. The Venezuelan sources distinguish retrospective party memory, current or recent party self-description, comparative theory, and independent party-system analysis; they do not establish uniform COPEI policy, equal inclusion, every government outcome, or a present-day Venezuelan score. The Portuguese sources distinguish founding programme, 1975 constitutional proposal, constituent and revision settlements, party self-description, coalition participation, Catholic hierarchy, later CDS–PP change, and independent interpretation. The Swiss and Portuguese sources distinguish national and cantonal or transition-specific timing, institutional history, party self-description, and independent interpretation. None of these cases establishes a complete implementation record, a present-day country classification, or a universal model. Scores are didactic composites and should not classify a party or country without dated programme and institutional evidence.',
