            text: 'The Religious Kibbutz Movement provides a bounded Jewish and Middle Eastern case that is neither secular Bundist socialism nor Christian liberation theology. Katz’s study follows its 1935–1948 credo through movement-archive evidence, while Kaye analyzes communities in Palestine/Israel that tried to combine socialism, Jewish nationalism, and Orthodox Jewish law. The movement’s current English statement describes Torah observance, equality of human value, women’s status, democratic life, labor, and social responsibility; this is dated organizational self-description, not proof of historical continuity or outcomes. The case shows how collective economic practice, religious law, national settlement, and democratic participation can coexist in tension without becoming a model for all Jewish, Israeli, Palestinian, or religious-socialist politics.',
            citations: citations([], ['katzReligiousKibbutzCredo1995', 'kayeReligiousSocialistsIsrael2022', 'religiousKibbutzMovementValues']),
