            text: 'Colombia supplies a bounded case in which Christian liberation moved from sociological and political critique toward revolutionary armed struggle. A Spanish-language 1965 interview record presents Christianity and revolution as a question for Colombian and Latin American socioeconomic conditions; Pérez Prieto places Camilo Torres alongside other Colombian pioneers and movements in the origins of liberation theology; Martínez Morales reconstructs his concern with social justice and the moral and strategic problem of violence. López’s French study documents the conflict with the Catholic hierarchy from 1962 to September 1965. The case therefore illuminates a real authority-and-method dispute without making Torres a proxy for every liberation theologian, Colombian Catholic, or socialist movement.',
