      editorialNote: 'The entry remains an umbrella synthesis with a predominantly Christian-centered evidence base, now including bounded Brazilian CEB, Emaús–CESEEP–Faith and Politics, South African Kairos, Colombian Camilo Torres, Buber, the 1935–1948 Religious Kibbutz case, and pre-1979 Iranian Islamic-left comparisons. The Religious Kibbutz addition separates Katz’s archive-based scholarly study, Kaye’s analysis of law and intellectual transmission, and the movement’s current organizational self-description; it is a Jewish communal case, not a classification of all Jewish, Israeli, Palestinian, or religious-socialist politics. It distinguishes religious socialism, Christian socialism, liberation theology, Catholic Worker practice, Black and contextual theology, Christian democracy, secular democratic socialism, anti-colonial liberation, Islamic revolutionary socialism, and theocracy. The Brazilian sources are not interchangeable: CELAM supplies restricted primary metadata; Krischke supplies a comparative abstract; Maués, Menezes Neto, and Meirelles supply Portuguese regional or movement studies; CNBB supplies official self-description and retrospective institutional memory; and Freire supplies a bounded Portuguese network history of political education and Christian activism. The Colombian addition is also layered: a 1965 Spanish interview record is represented by metadata and abstract, Pérez Prieto provides Spanish contextual scholarship, López provides a French study of the hierarchy conflict, and Martínez Morales analyzes Christianity, social justice, politics, and violence. The Colombian case is a bounded revolutionary trajectory, not a general theory of liberation theology or a causal rule about religious socialism. The Kairos cluster separates an archived primary document, Goba’s contemporaneous scholarly interpretation, and Mahlangu’s open-access later study; it does not make the document a complete socialist programme or classify present-day South Africa. Buber’s and Shariati’s selected English materials, scholarly interpretations, and publisher metadata also have different roles; none is empirical validation, and the later Islamic Republic is not treated as Shariati’s programme. Scores remain didactic composites, with existing article/card discrepancies still recorded. Broader non-Christian traditions, fuller Brazilian, Colombian, South African, and Jewish archives, and country-specific claims require separate research.',
