      editorialNote: 'This entry distinguishes liberal constitutionalism as a family of arguments for rights-constrained public power from classical liberal economics, progressive liberalism, civic nationalism, judicial supremacy, secularism, and formal constitutionalism without equal inclusion. Historical examples are comparative and partial; scores are didactic and should not be read as empirical measurements of every liberal constitutional party or state. The India addition is a bounded postcolonial case using an official constitutional link, an official Supreme Court summary, and selected academic readings; it does not establish a whole-country score, complete constitutional history, or equal rights in practice. The Cádiz addition is a bounded Spanish and transatlantic case using the official 1812 text, Spanish scholarship, the Universidad de Cádiz oath-record project, and a Cuban implementation study represented through metadata/abstracts; it now distinguishes constitutional design, local publication and oath practices, municipal and provincial institutions, representation conflicts, and old-regime continuities, without claiming uniform consent, equal citizenship, or a score for Spain or its former territories. The West German addition is a bounded post-authoritarian constitutional case: the Basic Law, court practice, guarded democracy, party competition, and social-market policy are related but non-identical evidence layers, and the current English consolidation is not a complete German-language constitutional history. The Brazil addition is a bounded Portuguese-language post-authoritarian case using the official 1988 text and abstract-level Brazilian scholarship on participation, social-rights conflict, judicial review and public-administration capacity; it does not establish a whole-country score, complete implementation history or settled resolution of the court-versus-administration debate. The French Third Republic addition is a bounded 1875–1940 parliamentary and intellectual case using official French parliamentary history, a French-language study, and two attributed scholarly interpretations; it does not establish universal rights, equal colonial citizenship, or an exact score for France. The French Restoration addition is a bounded 1814–1848 constitutional-monarchical case using French institutional histories and attributed legal-history scholarship. It distinguishes the royal grant, retained revolutionary liberties, appointed peerage, censitary franchise, changing ministerial responsibility and the 1830 revision; it does not establish popular sovereignty, universal suffrage, equal citizenship or complete implementation. The Mexican addition is a bounded Spanish-language Latin American case using the official historical record, a Library of Congress contextual account, an English convention history, and Spanish agrarian and labor scholarship; it distinguishes constitutional design, revolutionary genealogy, and implementation without assigning a present-day country score or treating social constitutionalism as one settled programme. The Ottoman addition is a bounded 1860s–1909 Constantinople-centered intellectual and constitutional case using a primary diplomatic witness, official parliamentary history, electoral scholarship, Namık Kemal research, and recent Ottoman-liberal thought scholarship. It distinguishes Ottoman-Islamic and European vocabularies, constitutional design, empire-wide implementation, communal and provincial participation, military power, and later Turkish republican trajectories; it does not turn a capital-city network into a uniform empire-wide ideology or a present-day country score. The Khayr al-Din addition is a bounded Arabic–Ottoman reform case using the 1867 Arabic treatise record, Peter Hill’s publisher abstract, and Odile Moreau’s French comparative article. It connects Arabic and Islamic political reasoning with questions about concentrated power, administrative capacity, and constitutional reform without merging Tunisian and Ottoman settings, assuming democratic implementation, or claiming a complete translation history. The Swiss Reformation addition is a bounded multilingual antecedent case using an official Swiss historical overview, a French Genevan archival transcription, Portuguese-language scholarship, and English scholarship on church–civil relations. It distinguishes civic approval, ecclesiastical offices, discipline, Consistory and Council practice, and confessional coercion from modern liberal rights and secular constitutionalism; the new Genevan detail separates civic ratification and office differentiation from popular sovereignty, equal membership and secular separation. No score is recalibrated and no complete Reformation historiography is claimed. The Swiss federal founding addition is a bounded 1847–1848 constitutional case using the Swiss Parliament’s archival witness, the Federal Archives’ institutional history, and Dardanelli and Mueller’s open scholarly study. It separates federal constitutional design, male suffrage, cantonal autonomy, gradual legislative centralization, and later rights expansion, without treating 1848 Switzerland as inclusive by present standards or assigning a present-day country score. The Ghana addition is a bounded West African case using the official 1992 constitutional text and rights chapter, Quashigah’s legal overview, Owusu’s account of the transition from PNDC rule, Gyampo and Graham’s critique of hybrid executive design, and Jacquemot’s French-language study of districts and customary chieftaincies. It keeps constitutional design, referendum and party transition, executive–legislative balance, rights enforcement, local plural authority and lived inclusion as separate evidence layers; it does not assign a Ghana-wide score or treat customary and elected institutions as uniformly complementary. The Bhe addition is a bounded 2004 South African rights-adjudication case using the Constitutional Court’s official judgment record and Knoetze and Olivier’s abstract-level legal analysis. It distinguishes equality and dignity review, apartheid-era statutory differentiation, customary-law rule, gendered succession and remedy without treating one court decision as a complete account of South African customary law, implementation, or present-day country quality. The Dufour addition is a bounded French-language interpretation of Rousseau’s Genevan anchoring, natural-law modeling and changing evaluation of Geneva’s institutions; it does not resolve the complete French corpus, reception history or whether Rousseau belongs inside liberal constitutionalism rather than as a contested precursor. The Belgian addition is a bounded 1830–1831 constitutional-monarchy case now strengthened by French primary, Dutch-language government and parliamentary archival, and Dutch KU Leuven sovereignty research. It adds evidence about national sovereignty, representative mediation, religious liberty, ministerial responsibility, bilingual source hierarchy and restricted census suffrage without changing the six-axis score or claiming that the Belgian settlement created universal popular rule.',
