            text: 'Russian expansion across the steppe and Central Asian khanates unfolded through successive military campaigns, frontier settlements, diplomacy and administrative consolidation rather than one single war. Bregel identifies the 1867 creation of the Turkestan governorate-general after the conquest of Tashkent and adjacent territories; later campaigns extended Russian control toward Bukhara, Khiva, the Turkmen lands and the Pamirs. The 1874 diplomatic record on Zarafshan, after the 1868 capture of Samarkand, describes commander-led administration and a budget dominated by military expenditure. Pierce treats the 1867–1917 period as colonial rule involving law, taxation, education, commerce, native elites and Islamic institutions, while Morrison’s Samarkand comparison asks which features were distinctively Russian and which resembled other European empires. The sequence demonstrates imperial militarism without proving a single motive, uniform local response, or identical status for annexed territories and protectorates.',
